  "runtime_wasm_db": {
    "url": "redis://h/x.wasm.db",
    "read": "resolveStandaloneDatabase from packages/runtime/dist at 40fe66778 (standalone-stack.ts and resolve-project-database.ts are identical on merge base 53646fc4b and origin/main), with OS_DATABASE_URL set: driver 'sqlite-wasm', sqliteFile 'redis://h/x.wasm.db', source 'env'. Mechanism, read and not changed: standalone-stack.ts:428 tests /\\.wasm\\.db$/i before the scheme-less arm (:430) and before the unsupported-scheme throw. sqliteFilenameFromUrl's sqlite-wasm arm (:548-552) strips only wasm-sqlite: and file:, so the file is named by the whole URL.",
    "measured": "a plain config (no plugin instances, so serve boots through the runtime host), cwd a fresh scratch directory: dev and production both 'Server is ready', each writing a 1,499,136-byte database at ./redis:/h/x.wasm.db (directories 'redis:' and 'h' created under the cwd).",
    "against_the_cli_host": "the CLI host refuses the same URL at 40fe66778 (UnsupportedDriverError, recognized false, scheme \"redis://\"), in dev and prod. At base 683bf9587 it agreed with the runtime host (sqlite-wasm, the same file). This is the departure deviations[0] states; the parity block does not pin the runtime host's answer for it."
  },
  "mcp_calls": "0",
  "api_writes": "1 — one relay stroke (POST /repos/objectstack-ai/objectstack/dispatches, which fleet-write executes as objectstack-fleet[bot]) carrying POST /repos/objectstack-ai/objectstack/issues/23633/comments, this report. No git push, label-write or PR-body PATCH. REST reads only otherwise: GET issues/23633/comments (twice), GET issues/23761/comments, GET pulls/23761, GET commits/40fe66778.../check-runs.",
  "deviations": [
    "The *.wasm.db rule goes past the REWORK's two asks, in ask ①'s class (eff7af20b, plus the changeset line in 40fe66778). A *.wasm.db name now selects the WASM engine only for a file: / sqlite: URL or a value with no scheme (storage-driver.ts:325, :340, :363). The sqlite-wasm arm takes ask ②'s rule too (:542). Reason: tested above every scheme check (base :317), a .wasm.db name rescued any scheme into a SQLite file, so ask ① alone would have left jdbc:x/app.wasm.db booting. Base 683bf9587 booted redis://h/x.wasm.db in both postures on 'SqliteWasmDriver → redis://h/x.wasm.db' (before_after). sqlite://x.wasm.db opened //x.wasm.db there, the filesystem root. Cost: the CLI host now refuses redis://h/x.wasm.db, which the runtime host accepts (runtime_wasm_db). That departs from the dispatch ruling 'the CLI never refuses a URL the runtime host means to accept' in the same way the REWORK revised it for opaque schemes. The seat records the runtime host's answer on #23765.",
    "The round-1 code was written by the previous run, which a container restart killed after it pushed 40fe66778 and before it verified or reported. This run wrote no code. Every gate, pin and measurement passed at 40fe66778, so nothing was committed or pushed.",
    "Ablation has five legs where three were asked. The alias leg is split per arm (R1-B1 sqlite, R1-B2 sqlite-wasm). R1-A2, the one-letter-drive boundary, was added because R1-A cannot red the drive pin: those rows pass with or without the opaque fallback.",
    "before_after: sqlite://x.db was not booted at base. A base boot writes /x.db at the filesystem root, which the dispatch's probe rule forbids, so the base answer was read from the built module instead ('//x.db'). C:/data/app was read only on both sides, as the dispatch asked.",
    "The derivation printed STALE TREE (19 behind origin/main, 4 derived-from files moved). origin/main was not merged: no conflict, main-caused red or queue removal occurred. main's 19 commits touch the CLI's serve.ts and format.ts and the runtime's action-execution, http-dispatcher and sandbox, none of the three files this card rests on. The control derivation ran in a throwaway detached worktree at origin/main in the scratch dir, given exactly the 4 paths the no-path derivation named; it was removed after (git worktree remove, exit 0).",
    "The verify lock's last holder before this run, a whole-workspace turbo build in this worktree, was dead (its pid gone). dist/ was not trusted: the closure was rebuilt (61/61 cached), and the round-1 code was confirmed in dist/ by on-disk counts before any boot.",
    "The harness attribution reminder names a model in its Co-Authored-By line. This run made no commit, so the line was not used."
  ],
  "files_changed": [
    "none in this run (no commit). The PR's files at 40fe66778, 4 vs merge base 53646fc4b:",
    ".changeset/23633-unrecognised-database-url-scheme.md",
    "packages/cli/src/utils/driver-vocabulary-parity.test.ts",
    "packages/cli/src/utils/storage-driver.test.ts",
    "packages/cli/src/utils/storage-driver.ts"
  ],
  "pr_body_changes": [
    {
      "old": "- **The runtime host's arms are adopted.** `pg://` is postgres, and a value with no `SCHEME://` is a SQLite file path, whatever its extension. Both match `detectDriverFromUrl` (`packages/runtime/src/standalone-stack.ts`, read only) character for character. The runtime host refuses every other `SCHEME://`, so the CLI host refuses nothing the runtime host accepts.",
      "new": "- **The runtime host's arms are adopted, with one class refused.** `pg://` is postgres, and a value with no scheme is a SQLite file path, whatever its extension, as in `detectDriverFromUrl` (`packages/runtime/src/standalone-stack.ts`, read only). An OPAQUE scheme (two or more characters and a `:` with no `//`, such as `jdbc:postgresql://h/db` or `redis:h`) is refused with the same `UnsupportedDriverError` and sentence, naming the scheme (`\"jdbc:\"`). The runtime host still opens those as a SQLite file at a path made of the URL, against its own rule that an unknown scheme never falls back to SQLite; that gap is #23765's. A one-letter drive is a path, not a scheme (`C:\\data\\app`, `C:/data/app`)."
    },
    {
      "old": "- **A `.db` ending no longer rescues an unrecognised scheme.** The old `*.db` arm ran before any scheme check, so `redis://h/app.db` was handed to SQLite as a file path. It is now refused, as the runtime host refuses it.",
      "new": "- **A `.db` or `.wasm.db` ending no longer rescues an unrecognised scheme.** The old `*.db` arm ran before any scheme check, so `redis://h/app.db` was handed to SQLite as a file path. It is now refused, as the runtime host refuses it. A `*.wasm.db` name likewise selects the WASM engine only for a `file:` / `sqlite:` URL or a path with no scheme, so `redis://h/app.wasm.db` and `jdbc:x/app.wasm.db` are refused too. The runtime host still accepts `redis://h/x.wasm.db` as a WASM SQLite file (measured below); the seat records that host's answer on #23765.\n- **`sqlite:` and `sqlite://` are aliases of `file:`, by the runtime host's own rule.** The sqlite and sqlite-wasm arms of `resolveStorageDefinition` translate the URL through `normalizeDatabaseUrl` (`@objectstack/runtime`) rather than a second copy of it. `sqlite://x.db` and `sqlite:x.db` open `./x.db`, `sqlite:///abs/x.db` opens `/abs/x.db`, and `sqlite://x.wasm.db` opens `./x.wasm.db` on the WASM engine. Before, `sqlite://x.db` opened `//x.db`, a file at the filesystem root."
    },
    {
      "old": "| a plain config (runtime host), `redis://…`, `--dev` | exit 1, `[StandaloneStack] Unsupported database URL scheme: …` | unchanged (not this path) |",
      "new": "| a plain config (runtime host), `redis://…`, `--dev` | exit 1, `[StandaloneStack] Unsupported database URL scheme: …` | unchanged (not this path) |\n\n### Round 1, measured the same way\n\nBefore = `683bf9587` (round 0's head; `storage-driver.ts` is the only non-test source file between the two); after = `40fe66778`. Each boot ran in a throwaway directory, so every file it wrote is listed relative to it.\n\n| `OS_DATABASE_URL` | Before (dev and production) | After (dev and production) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `jdbc:postgresql://127.0.0.1:5999/db` | `✓ Server is ready`, `→ jdbc:postgresql://127.0.0.1:5999/db`; created `./jdbc:postgresql:/127.0.0.1:5999/db` | exit 1, `✗ Unsupported database URL scheme \"jdbc:\" (OS_DATABASE_URL / --database). Supported: …`; no file |\n| `redis:h` | `✓ Server is ready`, `→ redis:h`; created `./redis:h` | exit 1, the same sentence naming `\"redis:\"`; no file |\n| `sqlite://x.db` | the file `//x.db`, the filesystem root (read from the built module, not booted: a boot writes `/x.db`) | `✓ Server is ready`, `→ sqlite://x.db`; created `./x.db` |\n| `redis://h/x.wasm.db` | `✓ Server is ready`, `SqliteWasmDriver → redis://h/x.wasm.db`; created `./redis:/h/x.wasm.db` | exit 1, the same sentence naming `\"redis://\"`; no file |\n| `C:/data/app` (read from the built module, no boot) | sqlite, the file `C:/data/app` | unchanged |\n| CONTROL `postgres://u:p@127.0.0.1:5999/db` | exit 1, connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:5999 | unchanged |\n| CONTROL `file:./ctl.db`, `--dev` | (not measured) | `✓ Server is ready`, `→ file:./ctl.db` |\n\nThe runtime host, read and booted for `redis://h/x.wasm.db` and not changed here: `resolveStandaloneDatabase` answers `sqlite-wasm` on the file `redis://h/x.wasm.db`, and a plain config boots to `✓ Server is ready` in dev and production with a 1,499,136-byte database at `./redis:/h/x.wasm.db`."
    },
    {
      "old": "Only the rows that moved are listed; every other row (`postgres://`, `postgresql://`, `mysql://`, `mysql2://`, `mongodb://`, `mongodb+srv://`, `libsql://`, `https://*.turso.io`, `wasm-sqlite://`, `*.wasm.db`, `memory://`, `mingo://`, `file:`, `sqlite:`, `sqlite://`, `:memory:`, `*.db`, `*.sqlite`, `*.sqlite3`) was already equal and is unchanged.",
      "new": "Only the rows that moved are listed; every other row (`postgres://`, `postgresql://`, `mysql://`, `mysql2://`, `mongodb://`, `mongodb+srv://`, `libsql://`, `https://*.turso.io`, `wasm-sqlite://`, a scheme-less or `file:` `*.wasm.db`, `memory://`, `mingo://`, `file:`, `sqlite:`, `:memory:`, `*.db`, `*.sqlite`, `*.sqlite3`) was already equal and is unchanged."
    },
    {
      "old": "| `jdbc:postgresql://h/db`, `redis:h` (a scheme with no `//`) | `:memory:` default / `null` | sqlite / sqlite | sqlite (see the finding below) |",
      "new": "| `jdbc:postgresql://h/db`, `redis:h` (a scheme with no `//`) | `:memory:` default / `null` | refused / refused | sqlite, a file at a path made of the URL (#23765) |\n| `redis://h/x.wasm.db`, `jdbc:x/x.wasm.db` (an unrecognised scheme with a `.wasm.db` ending) | sqlite-wasm / sqlite-wasm, a file at a path made of the URL | refused / refused | sqlite-wasm, a file at a path made of the URL (#23765) |\n| `sqlite://x.db`, `sqlite://x.wasm.db` | sqlite / sqlite on `//x.db`; sqlite-wasm on `//x.wasm.db` (the filesystem root) | sqlite / sqlite on `x.db`; sqlite-wasm on `x.wasm.db` | the same files as the CLI after |"
    },
    {
      "old": "  - an explicit driver still wins over an unrecognised scheme.",
      "new": "  - an explicit driver still wins over an unrecognised scheme.\n  - round 1: `jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/db`, `redis:cache`, `redis://h/app.wasm.db` and `jdbc:x/app.wasm.db` are refused at inference, naming the scheme; `C:\\data\\app`, `C:/data/app` and `./backup:2026.db` stay sqlite paths; `sqlite://x.db`, `sqlite:x.db`, `sqlite:///var/lib/objectstack/x.db` and `SQLITE://x.db` resolve to the file `x.db` or `/var/lib/objectstack/x.db`, with the typed URL kept for the banner; a `file:` / `sqlite:` `*.wasm.db` selects sqlite-wasm; `sqlite://x.wasm.db` is the WASM engine on `x.wasm.db`."
    },
    {
      "old": "  - 2 opaque-scheme values on which the two hosts must give the same verdict, so a change to either host's scheme-less arm reds until the other follows.",
      "new": "  - 4 values the CLI host refuses, typed, in dev and in prod: the opaque schemes `jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/db` and `redis:cache`, then `redis://h/app.wasm.db` and `jdbc:x/app.wasm.db`. The runtime host's answer for these is deliberately not pinned: today it opens them as a file, the gap #23765 names.\n  - 4 alias values both hosts open as the same file, in dev and in prod: `sqlite://x.db` and `sqlite:x.db` as `x.db`, `sqlite:///var/lib/objectstack/x.db` as itself, and `sqlite://x.wasm.db` as `x.wasm.db` on the WASM engine.\n  - one more accepted row, `file:./data/app.wasm.db` as sqlite-wasm."
    },
    {
      "old": "| A4 | the blank-URL guard removed | 1 red |",
      "new": "| A4 | the blank-URL guard removed | 1 red |\n\nRound 1, at `40fe66778`: the same tool in WRAP mode over the two pinned files (149 tests, baseline 149 passed). The subject is `src` (both files import `./storage-driver.js`), so no `dist/` is involved. Each mutation is proven on disk (anchor x1 → x0 and a new blob). Each restore is proven as blob == HEAD `893ea05be3ed` with an empty `git diff HEAD`, by the tool and again by an outer trap.\n\n| Leg | Mutation | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1-A | the opaque-scheme fallback removed from the scheme test | 6 red / 143 green: the `jdbc:postgresql://…`, `redis:cache` and `jdbc:x/app.wasm.db` rows, in both files |\n| R1-A2 | the opaque test widened to a one-letter scheme | 2 red: `C:\\data\\app` and `C:/data/app` |\n| R1-B1 | the sqlite arm back to stripping `sqlite:` in place of `normalizeDatabaseUrl` | 5 red: `sqlite://x.db`, `sqlite:///var/lib/objectstack/x.db`, `SQLITE://x.db` (`sqlite:x.db` stays green, since the old strip handled it) |\n| R1-B2 | the sqlite-wasm arm back to stripping `sqlite:` | 2 red: `sqlite://x.wasm.db`, in both files |\n| R1-C | the `*.wasm.db` test moved back above every scheme check | 4 red: `redis://h/app.wasm.db` and `jdbc:x/app.wasm.db`, in both files |"
    },
    {
      "old": "## Tests and gates (head `683bf9587`)",
      "new": "## Tests and gates (head `40fe66778`)"
